messages:
Marta: Quick question — we need a clear SDK stance for HTTP in JS land: node (CJS/ESM), edge runtimes, and bundlers. Who wants to own a recommended approach? :thinking:
Omar: I can take a stab. TL;DR proposal: prefer global fetch when present (Edge, Node>=18), fall back to a very small runtime-detect + dynamic import of undici for older Node. Document the types you need in TS.
Elaine: Agreed on prefer-global. Two DX pain points: 1) typings — consumers hit missing DOM lib errors if they use node-only tsconfigs, 2) bundlers (vite/webpack) sometimes polyfill node:undici weirdly.
Sam: Also remember ESM/CJS packaging. Consumers using CJS require() in node14/16 can break if we only ship ESM with top-level import('undici'). We should ship a compat entry that does runtime require of undici in CJS.
Marta: Proposed runtime detection snippet for the SDK (pseudo):
```
function getFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  try {
    // only require/import when needed to avoid bundler/edge issues
    // CJS path: require('undici').fetch
    // ESM path: await import('undici').then(m => m.fetch)
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('No fetch available. Please provide a fetch implementation to Redwood SDK');
  }
}
```
Omar: Exactly — do NOT ship a hard dependency that forces undici into browser/edge bundles. Use dynamic import/require inside a tiny adapter. Also expose an SDK option so callers can pass their own fetch: new Redwood({ fetch: myFetch }).
Elaine: For TS: in our README recommend two fixes: 1) add "dom" to compilerOptions.lib or 2) install @types/node-fetch/undici typings and augment global fetch type with a declaration file. Example in docs: add a small "global-fetch.d.ts" snippet.
Sam: For packaging: exports map with conditional exports. Example snippet for package.json: 
```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
  },
  "./runtime-fetch": {
    "default": "./dist/runtime-fetch.mjs"
  }
}
```
Marta: How do we handle edge-only builds (where bundlers tree-shake the dynamic import)? We should provide a note: if you bundle for edge, rely on global fetch and mark runtime-fetch module as side-effect free so bundlers can drop it.
Omar: Also call out Node-tooling specifics: Node 18+ has global fetch but types may be behind "lib": ["es2022", "dom"] in TS. For SDK source we can include an optional types export for consumers: "types": "dist/index.d.ts" and keep runtime adapter untyped (or ship a tiny d.ts for it).
Elaine: UX for new users: onboarding snippet in README: minimal examples for 4 cases — browser/edge, node>=18, node<18 (CJS), node<18 (ESM bundler). Each with the exact import/require and tsconfig hint.
Sam: CI Bot: running quick compatibility tests on node16 + webpack and node18 + vite.
CI Bot: test results: node16-webpack OK w/ undici shim, node18-vite OK w/out shim. Full log: https://ci.internal/redwood-sdk/1234
Marta: Decisions from thread: 1) SDK should prefer global fetch, 2) include a tiny runtime adapter that does runtime-detect + dynamic require/import of undici only when needed, 3) expose a fetch override in constructor, 4) add README onboarding section with tsconfig and bundler notes, 5) ship CJS + ESM outputs and a conditional exports map.
Omar: I'll open a draft PR with runtime-adapter + tests and add a docs skeleton. Will ping for reviews.
Elaine: I'll write the TS notes and a sample global-fetch.d.ts file.
Sam: I'll add CI job for node16 webpack compatibility.
Marta: Awesome — thanks. Please make sure we add a short blurb about security for private deployments (don’t force undici in air-gapped builds). :shipit:
Omar: Opening PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/redwood-js-sdk/pull/451 (draft) — feedback welcome.
